Teams that move fast already run their checks locally, and agents do it more
than anybody, because an agent is already holding the checkout and the
toolchain. Running the suite it broke a minute ago is the cheapest thing
available to it.

Moving that work off a cloud runner costs you something unrelated to speed. The
runner was a shared reference: one machine, outside everybody's control, that
the whole team had agreed in advance to believe. Your own laptop has none of
that behind it.

An agent runs two of the three required suites, gets stuck on the third, and
reports that everything passed. A terminal printing green is repeating what the
agent chose to report. A signed statement carries the policy digest, the
pipeline digest and the per-suite results, and a reader can check each of those
against the tree.

On the repository that produced this post, the GitHub Actions job that verifies
an integrated candidate spends 5 seconds starting up, 14 seconds installing
dependencies and 17 seconds building workspace packages before it can run a
single check. Those are medians across 18 successful runs. On the machine that
wrote the code, where the install and the build are already sitting on disk, the
same checks take 4.5 seconds at the median. Setup outweighs work by about eight
to one. No published study splits a job into setup and test, so this split is
our own measurement of one repository.

thwip gives you four settings for how much of that shared reference you rebuild
on your own machine.

## The workload changed shape

The [Octoverse 2025 report](https://github.blog/news-insights/octoverse/octoverse-a-new-developer-joins-github-every-second-as-ai-leads-typescript-to-1/)
counts nearly a billion commits pushed to GitHub in 2025, up 25.1% year over
year, and 43.2 million pull requests merged in an average month, up 23%.
Underneath those totals, agents write in overlapping bursts on the same
repository, which changes what a merge gate has to do.

CircleCI's
[2026 State of Software Delivery](https://circleci.com/blog/five-takeaways-2026-software-delivery-report/)
looked at 28,738,317 workflows and found average daily workflow runs up 59% year
over year. Over the same period the main-branch success rate fell to 70.8%, the
lowest in over five years, and the median team's recovery time was 72 minutes.

[Xu, Subramanian and Karthik](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.04697v2) studied 33,596
agent-authored pull requests across 2,807 repositories. They found that 79.4% of
them overlapped in time with another agent-authored pull request on the same
repository, and inside a one-week window that rises to 95.0%. When the two pull
requests came from different agents, 41.7% of the pairs conflicted textually,
against 19.8% for pairs from one agent. Of the conflicted files, 84.4% were
source rather than dependency manifests.

Overlap that heavy means the branch a suite passed on is often a different tree
from the one that lands. [Sakib, Banik and
Jadliwala](https://arxiv.org/html/2607.12428v1) audited agent-authored pull
requests for security code smells and found at least one in 38.9% of them. They
also report that 81.1% of the genuine secret leaks in their corpus reached merge
undetected. Those numbers rule out answering the cost problem by loosening the
gate.

Our own repository sits inside this workload. Between 28 July and 3 August 2026,
agents wrote 524 commits across 131 merged pull requests, over 10 packages and 2
apps carrying 1,928 tests.

## What the literature attests

A 2026 survey led by [Yiqi Wang](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.04990) defines
execution provenance as the typed graph of an agent execution, and evidence
tracing as that graph projected onto evidence-support relations. Trust functions
apply to the graph. That framing is coherent, and most of the field is working
inside it.

Rashidi's [systematization](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.05743) collects
39 papers published between 2023 and 2026 into 17 categories: sandbox
isolation, capability and access control, policy enforcement, time-of-check to
time-of-use races, Model Context Protocol threats, identity delegation,
execution provenance, network egress control, static analysis of agent output.
Every one of them works on the agent itself, watching it run or limiting what it
can reach.

Policy-enforcement studies in that corpus show failure rates between 69% and 98%
of real denylists, and the isolation literature does not address them. Rashidi
names four other cross-cutting gaps. Nobody studies policy-authoring errors, and
benign out-of-scope agent actions occur in up to 17.1% of cases with no coverage
in the access-control work.

## The object under the signature

thwip attests the fixed point of the execution: the tree the run leaves behind.
You cannot replay an agent session at merge time, so a signed trace of one is
worth the integrity of whatever recorded it. A git tree is a fixed object.
Anybody can pull it down, re-run the suites, and watch the attached claim
reproduce or fail.

thwip throws the trace away. The in-toto subject is a git tree:

```json
"subject": [{ "name": "git-tree", "digest": { "sha1": "…" } }]
```

`packages/protocol/src/schema.ts` refuses any other evaluation mode, with a
comment that says why:

```ts
// A proof covers the head tree. A proof claiming to cover anything else is
// a proof this schema has no rules for, so it is not a proof.
const evaluationMode = git["evaluationMode"];
if (evaluationMode !== "head-tree") return fail("git.evaluationMode invalid");
```

That puts the agent outside the boundary where pass and fail get decided. An
agent can drive the CLI and open the pull request. An enrolled device key does
the signing, and the GitHub App evaluates the result.
None of the agent's runtime behaviour enters the decision, so constraining that
behaviour would add nothing to it.

The time-of-check to time-of-use category has no window to attack here, because
the object under check is an immutable content hash. The whole class of
state-validation races collapses into one reason code,
`SUBJECT_TREE_MISMATCH`, which fires when the tree the proof covers differs from
the tree GitHub is asked to merge.

{% product-shot src="/blog/fig-trace-vs-tree.svg" alt="An agent execution graph of tool calls and file writes, reduced to a single tree hash" caption="Figure 1. The typed execution graph the survey literature attests, reduced to the one object thwip signs. We throw away everything to the left of the hash on purpose." /%}

Every setting below names a tree, and the suites either pass on that tree or
they do not.

{% product-shot src="/blog/fig-toctou.svg" alt="Timeline showing a validation-to-use window in the observe model, and no window when the subject is a content hash" caption="Figure 2. Where the state-validation window opens when a check inspects mutable runtime state, and why binding to a tree hash removes it." /%}

## The dial

Four security profiles live in `packages/policy`. Your choice decides what
somebody has to take on faith for a merge to mean what it says.

**`fast` believes an enrolled device key.** The evidence is the proof already
sitting on the pull request head, signed before the branch was integrated.
Nothing observed the run. `requiresIntegratedRun` returns false here and under
no other profile, so the queue merges on that proof and spends no CI run at all.
What you are trusting is a key GitHub says belongs to a person with write
access, and that person's machine.

**`remote` moves that trust to GitHub.** A workflow the repository has pinned
runs the required suites in GitHub Actions against the integrated candidate, and
the queue refuses with `NO_PINNED_VERIFIER` when a profile claims that run and no
pinned verifier exists to establish it. GitHub performs the build and signs the
provenance, which puts this profile at SLSA Build L3 and costs a CI run on every
merge.

**`fresh` swaps it for an assignment and a receipt.** The queue signs an
assignment naming the candidate tree, the required suites and the policy behind
them, and offers it to a device that has claimed the turn. That machine verifies
the assignment against the keys thwip publishes before fetching anything, puts
the candidate on disk, runs the suites, and signs a receipt for what it ran. The
control plane then checks the receipt against the assignment it answers. The
integrated candidate has been run, on the developer's own hardware, with no
fallback into Actions.

**`audited` asks for both.** It is `fresh`, and then GitHub's own checks prove
every required suite a second time before the merge lands. Suites a project
already declared remote stay remote, so turning `audited` on never subtracts a
requirement.

Every profile except `fast` requires a run lease, good for 1800 seconds. That is
what the `run.lease` field in the predicate carries.

The step from `fast` to `fresh` buys a run against the integrated candidate with
no compute moving into the cloud. The assignment names a tree and the receipt
names the same tree, so the queue can accept a run it never saw.

## What a CI minute costs

GitHub [publishes its rates](https://docs.github.com/en/billing/reference/actions-minute-multipliers)
per runner minute, and rounds each job up to the whole minute, so 61 seconds
bills two.

| Runner | Rate | Against Linux |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Linux, 2-core x64 | $0.006 | 1x |
| Windows, 2-core x64 | $0.010 | 1.7x |
| macOS, 3 or 4-core | $0.062 | 10.3x |

What a repository then spends comes from Bouzenia and Pradel's
[study of GitHub Actions](https://software-lab.org/publications/icse2024_workflows.pdf)
at ICSE 2024, which mined 1.3 million workflow runs across 952 repositories over
30 months. Their dollars use the $0.008 Linux rate from March 2023, so they sit
high against today's $0.006.

| Measured | Figure |
| --- | --- |
| Mean VM time per job | 6.3 minutes |
| Share of resources on test and build | 91.2% |
| Paid-tier repository, per year | $504 mean, $282 IQR |
| Paid-tier pull request | $0.36 mean |
| Repositories using caching | 32.9%, saving 3.4% |

Run duration is skewed hard enough that a budget built on the median comes in
low. CircleCI's
[2026 report](https://circleci.com/blog/five-takeaways-2026-software-delivery-report/)
puts the median workflow at 2.2 minutes against a mean of 9.9. Its
[2025 edition](https://circleci.com/landing-pages/assets/2025-state-of-software-delivery-report.pdf)
gives the spread across more than 14 million workflows: 2m 43s at p50, 8m 7s at
p75, and past 25 minutes at p95.

Four inputs decide the bill: merge frequency, runs per merge, run duration and
runner class. Take a team merging 100 times a week, a slowest matrix leg of
twenty minutes, and two runs per merge, once on the branch and once on the
integrated candidate. That is 4,000 runner-minutes a week: $24 on Linux, near
enough $1,250 a year. The same minutes on macOS cost $248 a week, about $12,900
a year, which is why hybrid matrices sit on the open problems list below.

The second input is where agents change the arithmetic.
[Xu, Subramanian and Karthik](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.04697v2) found that
79.4% of agent-authored pull requests overlap in time with another one on the
same repository, and a merge queue answers that overlap by re-verifying the
integrated candidate, so the run on the branch is rarely the last run.

## Measurements

Everything below comes from this repository between 28 July and 3 August 2026,
out of the git history, the GitHub API and the published chain.

**Proof coverage.** 168 commits on `main` carry a `thwip-proof` trailer. Every
one of those envelopes decodes and parses against the shipped schema, all 168 of
them declaring `evaluationMode: head-tree`. Of the 132 merges, 128 carried a
proof on the pull request head. The 4 without one are pull requests #1, #2, #4
and #5, which merged before the App was enforcing.

**What is in a proof.** Evidence rides the commit as a git trailer,
`thwip-proof: v1.<base64url DSSE envelope>`, wrapping an
[in-toto](https://in-toto.io/) Statement v1 with predicate
`urn:thwip:attestation:ci-run:v1`. The envelope is
[DSSE](https://github.com/secure-systems-lab/dsse), signed by an OpenSSH ed25519
key that the signer has enrolled on GitHub as a **Signing** key. GitHub's
Authentication keys do not count for this, and a human has to register the
device. The predicate carries the tree OID and its parents, the base ref and
base sha, a policy digest and version, a pipeline digest and runner, the suite
results with durations, and the signer's key id and GitHub user id.

It also carries `networkMode` and `cacheMode`. The signer declares both about
its own posture. Nothing observes them, and the verifier records them as claims
nobody checked.

**Who checks it.** The thwip GitHub App owns the required `thwip` check and
[executes no repository code](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/blob/main/docs/adr/0001-app-native-proof-checks.md).
It reads GitHub for permission and key enrollment, fetches a signed fact
bundle, and fails closed across a fixed vocabulary of 29 reason codes. One of
those codes says only that the evidence has not settled yet. A handful of the
other 28: `SUBJECT_TREE_MISMATCH`, `SUBJECT_BASE_MISMATCH`,
`SIGNER_KEY_REVOKED`, `SIGNER_PERMISSION_INSUFFICIENT`,
`PIPELINE_DIGEST_MISMATCH`, `SUITE_NOT_PASSED`, `PROOF_EXPIRED`.

The concurrency finding above is why a head proof cannot stand on its own. A
merge queue holds the lane and records every transition as a signed record.
There are seven kinds of record, and each claims something specific. If the
queue verified an integrated candidate, the record for that merge cites the
verifier's result. Nothing gets cited when a merge goes through on the head's
own proof, because no verifier ran. `GET /v1/queue/records/:digest` returns the
exact bytes that were signed.

**Suite time at the edge.** 504 suite results, all passing.

| Suite | Median | p90 | Max |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| lint | 0.3s | 0.9s | 3.7s |
| typecheck | 2.2s | 3.6s | 6.5s |
| test | 1.7s | 4.6s | 10.7s |
| **all three** | **4.5s** | **8.8s** | **16.0s** |

**What it costs to rebuild the environment.** Across 18 successful candidate
verifications in Actions, the job spends 5 seconds at the median on runner setup
and checkout, 14 seconds on install, and 17 seconds building workspace packages.
That is 36 seconds before any suite starts, and the suites then take 24 seconds
in that environment. The same three suites on a developer machine, with
dependencies and build outputs already present, take 12.5 seconds. Those 36
seconds are what `fresh` skips, because the machine that already built the tree
is the machine that runs the suites against it.

{% product-shot src="/blog/fig-setup-vs-work.svg" alt="Bar comparison of 36 seconds of environment rebuild against 4.5 seconds of suite execution at the edge" caption="Figure 3. On this repository, re-verifying in the cloud spends most of its time rebuilding a state the edge machine already holds. `fresh` skips that rebuild." /%}

**The chain.** `node scripts/verify-chain.mjs 1315419165` walks the published
merge chain from outside thwip, needing no account or token. When this was
written it reported 57 links, 57 of 57 signatures verified against the service
keys at `/.well-known/thwip-keys`, and no findings. Of those records, 47 are
`reused-proof-merge`, 5 are `candidate-verified-merge`, 4 are
`external-transition`, and 1 is a `base-correction`. Merging this post adds
another link, so run the script yourself for a current count. The chain only
reached that clean state on the evening of 3 August, after being broken since
the morning of 2 August.

{% product-shot src="/blog/fig-chain-kinds.svg" alt="Bar chart of 57 chain records by kind: 47 reused-proof merges, 5 candidate-verified merges, 4 external transitions, 1 base correction" caption="Figure 4. Every record on this repository's chain, by what it claims. The 47 reused-proof merges are the dial sitting on `fast`. The four external transitions are the merges of 2 August, described below." /%}

**Posture, published as it is.** Every one of the 168 proofs used the
`command` runner instead of a content-addressed
[Dagger](https://dagger.io/) pipeline, with `networkMode: unrestricted` and
`cacheMode: read-write`, which is the weakest posture the schema permits.
Behind them were 2 signers holding 3 device keys, working under 3 distinct
pipeline digests and policy versions 1, 3, and 4.

## What this repository demonstrates

This repository is where thwip gets built, and it merges through thwip, so what
it offers is an existence proof. 168 envelopes decode and parse against the
shipped schema, 128 of 132 merges carried a proof on the head, and 57 of 57
chain signatures verify against the published keys under a script anybody can
run without an account.

`fresh` and `audited` landed during the week these numbers cover, which is why
the chain holds 5 candidate-verified merges against 47 that reused the head's
proof, with the dial sitting on `fast` for the rest of the week.

## What a proof does not buy

**A compromised signing device beats us.** An attacker holding an enrolled
private key can sign a well-formed proof over a tree whose suites never passed,
and the App will accept it. The App checks a signature and a subject, with no
view of the execution behind them. Discarding the trace costs us that. Under
`remote` and `audited`, GitHub runs the suites itself.

At the `fast` end we offer reproducibility and a public record. On any single
merge, an observer who watched the process knows something we do not. The record
pays off across a run of merges, because every proof binds to a content hash
anybody can check out and re-run, and a signer whose claims stop reproducing
shows up in public where a private runtime trace would stay hidden. None of that
helps on a quiet repository, since a signer with a handful of merges behind them
has given nobody enough to compare against.

The gap we cannot close is the first bad merge after a key compromise on a
profile with no second execution. Without observing the run we have no mechanism
for it, and it is listed under open problems below.

The rest of the bounds:

- thwip never fetches your source. The App and the control plane verify
  metadata, digests, and signatures.
- A green check says approved suites passed on a tree, signed by an identified
  key. A human still has to review the code for security, and the check says
  nothing about how the product behaves.
- Local and remote environments diverge. Work that needs the cloud as its
  execution environment should run there under `remote`.
- Identity is GitHub's. Membership and repository permission come from the
  organisation, and we add no second invite graph.
- A receipt under `fresh` is signed by the same class of device key that signs a
  proof under `fast`, so `fresh` narrows what a compromised device can claim
  while leaving it a threat.

## Check our chain

[`packages/queue-protocol`](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/tree/main/packages/queue-protocol)
exports `verifyChain` and ships with its tests. Given the published records, it
checks that each link is the digest of the record before it, then resolves every
citation each record makes and reports any record carrying a gap where a
citation belongs.

[`scripts/verify-chain.mjs`](https://github.com/recallnet/thwip/blob/main/scripts/verify-chain.mjs)
runs it against a live repository. It needs no thwip account:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/recallnet/thwip && cd thwip
pnpm install && pnpm build
node scripts/verify-chain.mjs recallnet/thwip
```

A merge that lands without a record leaves the base of one record short of the
next, which the walk reports as `BASE_DISCONTINUITY` and names the commit the
chain never accounted for. The queue runs this same verifier against its own
chain on every tick. If you break our chain, open an issue and paste what the
script printed, and we will act on it.

## Open problems

Ones we intend to close:

- Content-addressed pipelines as the default runner, so "same tree, same
  digests" comes out of the graph itself instead of resting on a convention.
- Hybrid matrices, where part of the suite has to stay remote for platform
  reasons.
- Enough `fresh` and `audited` merges to say something measured about them,
  rather than describing them from the code.

Ones we do not know how to close:

- Detecting the first bad merge after a device compromise under `fast`, without
  observing execution.
- Multi-party agreement on digests before a merge, at a cost a small team will
  pay.
- Whether `networkMode` and `cacheMode` can be made verifiable at the edge
  without reintroducing the sandbox literature's failure rates.

If you work on execution provenance and we have placed thwip wrongly in that
taxonomy, tell us and we will fix it. We would also like the citation for any
system that already does this and that we missed.

